                <h2>Background</h2>
<p>Southeast Asia and Latin America are top growth markets for overseas user acquisition, but localization complexity far exceeds expectations. Many teams follow a "translate and upload" approach, resulting in high CTR but poor conversion, or account bans due to compliance issues. This article breaks down localization into 6 actionable checkpoints for operations teams.</p>
<hr />
<h2>SOP Step 1: Language Localization—Rewrite, Don't Translate</h2>
<p><strong>Common Mistake</strong>: Direct machine translation or using Western copy leads to misaligned tone, slang, and cultural references.</p>
<p><strong>Execution Steps</strong>:</p>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>Distinguish official vs. colloquial language</strong>: Indonesia has 700+ dialects; prioritize Bahasa Indonesia for social media. Brazilian Portuguese differs significantly from European Portuguese—use BR localization.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Never literal-translate brand slogans</strong>: English "Don't miss out" could become "Don't be too greedy" in Thai, creating negative connotations.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Dual native-speaker review</strong>: After translation, require review by a local employee or outsourced translator, checking: politeness levels, number formats, currency units.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Checklist</strong>: □ Translated by native speaker □ No literal slogan translation □ Number/currency formats adapted □ Emoji compatibility verified</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>SOP Step 2: Payment Configuration—No Withdrawal Means Zero Revenue</h2>
<p><strong>Common Mistake</strong>: Assuming PayPal and credit cards are sufficient; payment ecosystems differ drastically.</p>
<p><strong>Execution Steps</strong>:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Regional payment essentials</strong>:</li>
<li>Indonesia: GoPay, OVO, DANA, Bank Transfer</li>
<li>Vietnam: MoMo, ZaloPay, VietQR</li>
<li>Brazil: Pix (&gt;60% of online payments), Boleto Bancário</li>
<li>
<p>Mexico: OXXO, SPEI</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Front-load payment options</strong>: Users drop off most at payment; keep selections within 2 clicks.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Test minimum purchase threshold</strong>: Offer low-price entry points (&lt;$5) for first purchase, then upsell.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Checklist</strong>: □ Local payment methods integrated □ Checkout path ≤2 clicks □ Minimum purchase flow tested □ Payment error messages localized</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>SOP Step 3: Budget &amp; Bidding—Don't Split Evenly</h2>
<p><strong>Common Mistake</strong>: Equal budget across 6 SEA countries, or applying Western CPM bids.</p>
<p><strong>Execution Steps</strong>:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Budget by market maturity</strong>:</li>
<li>Tier 1 (competitive): Singapore, Malaysia → reference Western CPMs</li>
<li>Tier 2 (growth): Indonesia, Thailand, Vietnam, Philippines → CPM 30-50% lower, CPC volatile</li>
<li>
<p>Tier 3 (emerging): Brazil, Mexico → separate Campaigns, avoid mixing with SEA</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Set regional bid modifiers</strong>: Meta supports country-level bid adjustments; set Indonesia 0.6-0.8, Brazil 0.7-0.9.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Timezone differentiation</strong>: SEA peak: 20:00-23:00 (UTC+7); LATAM peak: 19:00-22:00 (UTC-3), configure separate Schedules.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Checklist</strong>: □ Budget tiered by market □ Regional bid modifiers set □ Local peak hours configured □ Country-level CPM tracked independently</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>SOP Step 4: Creative Localization—Colors, Numbers &amp; Taboos</h2>
<p><strong>Common Mistake</strong>: Changing language but not visuals, or assuming "Asian face = SEA audience."</p>
<p><strong>Execution Steps</strong>:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Visual audit checklist</strong>:</li>
<li>Colors: Green has religious meaning in SEA; yellow carries negative connotations in parts of LATAM</li>
<li>Numbers: 4 in Vietnam, 13 in Brazil are unlucky—avoid in prices and promotions</li>
<li>
<p>People: Use local models/KOLs; match skin tone, hairstyle, attire to local aesthetics</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>UGC style adaptation</strong>: SEA users trust "real user sharing" content more; avoid over-polished ads. LATAM audiences accept humorous, enthusiastic messaging.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Prioritize vertical video</strong>: TikTok dominates SEA; Instagram Reels grows fast in LATAM. Prioritize 9:16 vertical assets; horizontal as supplement only.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Checklist</strong>: □ Color/number taboos reviewed □ People localized □ Style adapted by region □ Vertical content ≥70%</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>SOP Step 5: Compliance &amp; Risk Control—Account Ban Hotspots</h2>
<p><strong>Common Mistake</strong>: Applying domestic compliance standards overseas, or ignoring regional data laws.</p>
<p><strong>Execution Steps</strong>:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Dual-track GDPR/PDPA compliance</strong>:</li>
<li>EU GDPR extends to parts of SEA</li>
<li>Indonesia PDPA, Thailand PDPA recently生效落地页需明确数据收集声明</li>
<li>
<p>Brazil LGPD, Mexico LFPDPPP生效—privacy policies must display in local language</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Ad content red lines</strong>:</p>
</li>
<li>Indonesia: No alcohol, gambling ads</li>
<li>Vietnam: No political content, unapproved medical claims</li>
<li>
<p>Brazil: No comparative ads, unauthorized celebrity likeness</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Account security</strong>:</p>
</li>
<li>2FA mandatory</li>
<li>Account name, payment info, BM binding must match business license</li>
<li>Avoid frequent IP switching</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Checklist</strong>: □ Privacy policy localized □ Ad content reviewed for local regulations □ 2FA enabled □ BM info matches license</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Conclusion: Localization Is Continuous Iteration</h2>
<p>Localization isn't a one-time task. Review key metrics (CTR, CVR, CAC, LTV) monthly by region to catch early signals of localization failure. Monitor local holidays and shopping events (Ramadan, Carnival) to adjust creatives and budgets proactively.</p>
